      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on some year-end stuff for our manufacturing plant. Can you walk me through the straight-line depreciation calculation for a piece of equipment? It cost $150,000, has an estimated salvage value of $25,000, and a useful life of 10 years. Just need to see the steps to make sure my spreadsheet is right.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate straight-line depreciation, follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the depreciation method, which in this case is Straight-Line Depreciation.\n2. Subtract the salvage value from the cost ($150,000 - $25,000 = $125,000).\n3. Divide the result by the useful life (10 years).\n4. Calculate the annual depreciation by dividing the total cost minus salvage value by the number of years.\n\nFor example: ($125,000 ÷ 10) = $12,500. This means the equipment is depreciated by $12,500 each year.",
