    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that makes sense. so if we had a different scenario where the equipment doesn't lose value at a constant rate—like if it's way more productive in the first few years and then it just kinds of withers away or loses its edge—would we use a different method? how would that look compared to the straight-line one?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the equipment's value doesn't depreciate at a constant rate, you would use a different method called Accelerated Depreciation. This method accurately reflects the equipment's acceleration of revenue and decrease in value during its useful life.\n\nThere are several Accelerated Depreciation methods, such as the Double Declining Balance (DDB) or the Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System (MACRS). The most common one for manufacturing assets is the Macro-Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System, which allocates higher depreciation in earlier years and lower depreciation in later years.\n\nUsing a DDB method, the equipment's original cost would be divided into three categories: \n- 100% of the first year's depreciation ($25,000: $150,000)\n- 150% ofyear 2's depreciation ($18,750: $173,750: next year it's 100% /160,000 along those lines.), \n- 125% of year 3's depreciation, and so on",
      "source": "target",
